From caf-talk Caf Jul 29 01:00:01 1994
From: herb@halcyon.com (Herb Effron)
Newsgroups: alt.internet.services,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.org.cpsr.talk,alt.best.of.internet
Subject: Re: commercial use policies
Date: Sat, 23 Jul 1994 20:00:21 -0800
Message-ID: <herb-2307942000210001@bellevue-ip8.halcyon.com>

Ordinary people discovered BBSs and consumer information networks/services
when the cost of high speed modems (14.4 KBS) dropped to less than $100.
The natural outcome was young entrepreneurs satisfying a
previously-undiscovered market for inexpensive public dial-up connections
to the Internet.

I don't believe that 'commercial' adequately describes the current
phenomena. The term simply means an exchange of one thing for another, as
compensation.

We could just as well talk about 'barter', trading conversation in
newsgroups in units of "kehrkles"*, in place of talk about buying and
selling things for $$$.

It will make little difference whether historians look for parallels (of
the ensuing societal effect) with the invention of movable type or the
telephone. The Internet (and whatever will succeed it) is becoming a
principal line of communications just like rivers, roads, railroads,
shipping channels, etc.

Whatever you find people building alongside these lines of communications,
you are finding the same things being built alongside the Internet ... 
and it will continue with the many forms of its successors.

* kehrkles? Yes, kehrkles.

A "kehrkle" is a unit of _intellectual energy_. The term was coined by a
KING-FM (classic music) announcer in Seattle some years ago after finding
a relationship between the numbering of Mozart's works (e.g. K.467) and
the quality of the particular work.

He noted that most rock 'n roll does not have a value greater than 35
kehrkles. :-)


