	    RELIGIOUS HUMAN RIGHTS IN THE WORLD TODAY: 
		LEGAL AND RELIGIOUS PERSPECTIVES

		  An International Conference
			      at
			EMORY UNIVERSITY

			Atlanta, Georgia
		       October 6-9, 1994

		---------------------------------

			  Convened by
	The Law and Religion Program at Emory University

			 Sponsored by
		  The Pew Charitable Trusts

		---------------------------------

THIS CONFERENCE brings together fifty distinguished speakers from around 
the world to take the measure and test the meaning of religious human 
rights.  Archbishop Desmond M. Tutu, Dr. Martin E. Marty, and Judge John 
T. Noonan, Jr., will offer keynote addresses.  Clerics, politicians, 
academics, and activists -- of all manner of religious persuasion and 
political experience -- will share the same stage and tell their stories.

     The conference considers four main themes.  Session One explores the 
history and structure of religious rights, and examines their place in 
the human rights pantheon.  Session Two treats the religious dimensions 
of religious rights -- in the discourse of sacred texts, the activism of 
religious organizations, and the practices of religious polities.  
Session Three takes up the legal dimensions of religious rights -- the 
religious-rights provisions of international and local law, and the 
patterns of abridgement and enforcement of these rights in selected 
nations and regions.  Session Four seeks to integrate the theory and law 
of religious rights through lectures and a concluding panel discussion.

